# HOW MANY COMPILATIONS FIT IN THIS MACHINE AT ONCE — one arithmetic, read by
# `.#gate`, `.#most`, `.#quick`, `.#sized-build` and `ronoctua-machine apply`.
#
# # The defect this replaces
#
# The arithmetic lived in `nix/gate.nix`, and only the batteries had it. A
# machine applying its own flake (`ronoctua-machine apply`: the update, the
# rescue's rebuild, the software centre's apply) ran `nix build` with
# `max-jobs = auto` and `cores = 0` — every core, times every core. With the
# Rust build in one derivation per compilation unit (`nix/rust/units.nix`,
# some 300 units in a system's build) that is a dozen `rustc`, each with a
# dozen threads, on a laptop: the shape that has `earlyoom` or the kernel kill
# a compiler with no message. So the arithmetic moved here, and every place
# that builds the unit graph asks it.
#
# # Why `rustc` dies with no message
#
# It is not the build and it is not memory pressure inside the sandbox. It is
# `earlyoom` on the HOST, which sends SIGTERM when available memory falls below
# its threshold and has `rustc` in its `--prefer` list — so `rustc` is chosen
# first, by configuration — or `systemd-oomd`, or the kernel's OOM killer. Nix
# reports the signal, the compiler never gets to print anything, and the
# failure looks like a compiler bug.
#
# `--max-jobs` alone does not save it: that caps DERIVATIONS in parallel, and a
# derivation of ours hands its compiler `$NIX_BUILD_CORES` jobserver tokens.
# What has to be capped is the product.
#
# # The arithmetic, from measurements and not from taste
#
# The floor is `earlyoom`'s trigger — 10% of total memory in the configuration
# measured here, which is also its default — plus a reserve, because the
# browser and the editor go on allocating while the build runs. What is left
# of what is AVAILABLE, never past the ceiling of the cgroup the builds run in,
# is the budget.
#
# # Each unit by its own class
#
# One slot for every compiler — the worst one's peak — kept the budget, and
# wasted it: of some 2300 units measured, 2200 peak under 640 MiB and only a
# score past 1 GiB (`metrics/peak-kib`, 2026-10-19), so a hub edit built four
# small compilers at a time on a twelve-processor machine with room for ten
# (F5: 1531 s, the same as the one workspace it replaced). So every unit has a
# CLASS, measured (`nix/rust/unit-classes.json`, the one place a unit's class
# is written, regenerated by `nix run .#rust-peaks -- --write`): light, medium
# and large are slots of their own size, built at one core beside others, and
# heavy is a unit no slot describes — the shell's program, one thin-LTO link
# of the whole footer —, built alone. A unit is put in the first class whose
# slot holds its measured peak and a tenth more (`headroomPercent`): one that
# grows a little stays inside its slot, and only `.#rust-peaks` moves it. It
# is refused only past the slot itself: the tenth is the margin between the
# measurement that wrote its class and the next one, which judges it.
#
# A unit's peak is its WHOLE build's, of what the budget pays for — the
# anonymous memory, shmem, swap and page tables of the builder and every
# process below it, sampled (`nix/scripts/rust-unit.py`): a unit that links
# runs `rustc` and the linker at once, and the budget is taken from
# `MemAvailable`, which counts their file pages as available page cache —,
# and it says the cores it was built with. A peak depends on them, so a unit
# is classed only by a measurement at the cores its class builds it with:
# one, for a class with a slot. A heavy unit measured alone at its own cores
# stays heavy; measured at one core, it is classed like any other.
#
# A unit the data does not name is medium until measured — except a
# third-party unit a lock change renamed: its id ends in a digest of what it
# is built with (`rust-plan.py`'s `key`: its features, its profile, its
# target's kind, crate types and edition, and the ids of its dependencies),
# so bumping `proc-macro2` renames half the dependencies, the same crates at
# the same versions. Such a unit CARRIES the class of the unit whose id
# differs from its own only by that digest (the heaviest, were there
# several): the same crate at the same version, target, mode and opt level
# — the same source. Whatever else changed is inside the digest, and
# nothing here tells what: after a lock change it is the dependencies, but
# the features or the profile may have changed too. The headroom holds a
# peak a little past the one measured; the measured check
# (`unit-classes-fit-the-measures`) judges the unit by its OWN measurement
# once it is built (its own `metrics`, against the class it carried) and
# refuses it if it did not fit. Every build says how many units it plans by
# a carried class and how many by none.
#
# # Nix weighs nothing, so the units are started one by one
#
# Nix has no weight per derivation: `--max-jobs` counts derivations, and one
# `nix build` of the unit graph would build a medium unit wherever a light
# one fits. Waves of one class each, built one after another, were measured
# against the build of a hub edit and lost to the single slot: every wave
# waits for its slowest unit, and the classes alternate up the graph
# (simulated on F5's own units and times: 586 to 680 s for the release set,
# against 500 s with one slot). So `sized_units` schedules the units itself:
# each is its own `nix build` (`--max-jobs 1`), started once every unit it is
# built from has been built, when its slot — and the few MiB of its own
# `nix` (`clientMiB`) — fits what is left of the budget beside the slots of
# what runs, never more at once than processors, the longest chain above it
# first. A heavy unit starts when nothing else can, and nothing starts beside
# it. The sum of the slots of what runs together never passes the budget,
# and every unit peaks under its slot (`.#rust-peaks`, and the check
# `unit-classes-fit-the-measures`, refuse one that does not): that is the
# bound on the memory of the build.
#
# What a unit is built from that is no unit (a C library, a table, a source
# that is a derivation) is built BEFORE the units, sized as what is no unit:
# left to a unit's own `nix build`, at one core and in the unit's slot, it
# was built there whole — `libghostty-vt`, whose `zig build` used every
# processor: 2365 MiB of its processes at one core, in a light unit's
# 768 MiB. What that is comes from the caller's own dry run, which listed
# it beside the units; the store's references say which of it the units
# are built from, and no second dry run asks Nix again.
#
# What is no unit (a NixOS system, a VM test, a C library, a link farm) is
# sized as before, `--max-jobs <jobs> --cores <cores>`: jobs at most four and
# `jobs × cores ≤ slots` of the general peak (`peakMiB`), because a
# derivation of someone else's usually uses every core it is given, and past
# four the wall-clock gain is small and the memory cost is not.
#
# # The plan carries its own numbers
#
# A machine's apply builds the plan of the NEW distro (`ronoctua.machine.
# phases`, evaluated from the machine's flake) with the program of the
# RUNNING one (`ronoctua-build-units`). A class table in the program would be
# the old version's: a class the new distro added is no class to it, and a
# slot it resized is charged at the old size. So every line of a plan says
# what the arithmetic needs — the slot in MiB, or `alone` with the heavy
# unit's own numbers — and the class's name is only said. The program holds
# no table of classes, and a newer plan is built by its own numbers.
#
# # The heavy ones are paid once, not per core
#
# A heavy unit has one compiler no division describes: the shell's program
# under `release` is one thin-LTO link of the whole footer. It does not split
# into slots, so it is paid once, and every OTHER core pays what was measured
# beside it: `shellMiB` at one core, `besideMiB` for each core more. The cores
# a heavy unit gets are what the budget leaves room for, and nothing else
# builds beside it.
#
# # Where the builds run decides the ceiling
#
# A person's `nix build` hands its builds to `nix-daemon`, whose cgroup has the
# ceiling: on this distro `ronoctua.memory.buildCeiling` (`MemoryHigh`, half the
# RAM by default), past which the kernel throttles every compiler and
# `systemd-oomd` kills the build. ROOT's `nix` opens the store itself and
# builds in its own process — `ronoctua-machine apply`, under the update's
# unit — so its ceiling is that unit's cgroup and its parents'. Sized by the
# daemon's when the builds run elsewhere, the budget would be someone else's.
{
  lib,
  writeText,
  writeShellApplication,
  coreutils,
  gawk,
  gnugrep,
  gnused,
}:

let
  # THE NUMBERS, each with where it was measured.
  numbers = {
    # THE GENERAL PEAK, the slot of a derivation that is no unit (a system,
    # a VM test's driver, a C library). `earlyoom`'s own log on this
    # project: 1577, 958 and 857 MiB of `VmRSS` for the three `rustc` it
    # killed (2026-09); the worst of those with margin. `libghostty-vt`'s
    # `zig build` at one core, its processes' `VmRSS` summed: 1488 MiB with
    # `-j` (`nix/ghostty-vt.nix`), 2365 without (2026-10-19).
    peakMiB = 1792;
    # THE CLASSES OF THE UNITS, lightest first, each with its slot: what one
    # compilation of the class is planned at, at one core. LIGHT AND MEDIUM
    # were chosen on F5's measures (2026-10-19, 2310 units of this tree:
    # `metrics/peak-kib`, then the largest child's `ru_maxrss`, file pages
    # included; at one core most of them, some third-party ones at the more
    # cores of an earlier build, which `metrics` did not say — the release
    # `cranelift-codegen` read 1562 MiB, and its RSS is 2098 at one core):
    # 2116 at or under 512 MiB, 2252 under 768. Simulated on F5's own hub
    # edit (its units, its times, this machine's budget), light at 768 and
    # medium at 2048 did best of 640/768/1024 by 1792/2048: a lighter light
    # slot puts more units in the medium one, a heavier one fits fewer at
    # once. Measured now by what the budget pays for (M1; the cold rebuild
    # of 2026-10-20, 2319 units at one core): 2058 at or under 256 MiB, 2253
    # under 512, 2284 light, the heaviest medium the player's release library
    # at 1421 — lighter than what the slots were simulated on, and they were
    # not simulated again (docs/PENDING.md).
    #
    # LARGE is what no medium slot holds with the headroom, built at one core
    # BESIDE others: the release `cranelift-codegen` (1931 MiB) and the
    # footer's release library (1902). Heavy, the library was built alone and
    # closed the release with the shell's program (65 s of one thread with
    # eleven cores idle, S1). The slot was chosen when the library's peak was
    # measured as PSS, 2296 MiB (2526 with the headroom); with the headroom
    # now the heavier of the two is 2124, and 2816 leaves it room to grow —
    # see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, section "O pico é do build inteiro (M1)".
    classes = [
      {
        name = "light";
        slotMiB = 768;
      }
      {
        name = "medium";
        slotMiB = 2048;
      }
      {
        name = "large";
        slotMiB = 2816;
      }
    ];
    # A unit goes to the first class whose slot holds its peak and this much
    # more, and is refused only past the slot itself: the measurement that
    # wrote its class and the next one may differ by this much (two builds of
    # one unit differ by less, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, section "O pico é do build inteiro (M1)").
    headroomPercent = 10;
    # The class of a unit the data does not name, until it is measured.
    unclassed = "medium";
    # EVERY UNIT ITS OWN `nix build`: the client and the daemon's worker
    # serving it. Measured: `nix build` of one unit at 43 MiB of `ru_maxrss`
    # (Nix 2.35.1), the worker a few MiB of its own.
    clientMiB = 48;
    # THE HEAVIEST HEAVY UNIT AT ONE CORE, and what each core more adds beside
    # it — `metrics/peak-kib` of each heavy unit built alone at 1, 3 and 6
    # cores (2026-10-15, this tree, a 12-processor machine):
    #
    #   the shell's test harness       4350, 4717, 5175 MiB   165 MiB a core
    #   the shell's release program    3847, 4359, 4985 MiB   228 MiB a core
    #                                  (thin LTO, sixteen codegen units)
    #
    # the worst at one core and the steepest slope, with margin. Crane's
    # workspace, one codegen unit, measured 5120 MiB for the shell at four
    # cores and 846 MiB a core beside it (2026-09-27): the 5632 and 1024 this
    # replaces. With 10.6 GiB free on that machine they gave a heavy unit
    # three cores; these give it eleven, and the measured slope puts it at
    # about 6 GiB there, inside the 8 GiB budget. Written into every heavy
    # line of a plan (`alone:<shellMiB>+<besideMiB>`).
    shellMiB = 4864;
    besideMiB = 320;
    # Kept free on top of `earlyoom`'s floor.
    reserveMiB = 1024;
    # `earlyoom -m 10,5`: SIGTERM at 10% of total.
    floorPercent = 10;
    # Past this the batteries' general builds gain little (see above).
    jobsCeiling = 4;
  };

  classNames = map (c: c.name) numbers.classes;
  slotOf = lib.listToAttrs (map (c: lib.nameValuePair c.name c.slotMiB) numbers.classes);
  # Every class, lightest first, by its place: the heavier of two is the one
  # further on.
  rank = lib.listToAttrs (lib.imap0 (i: c: lib.nameValuePair c i) (classNames ++ [ "heavy" ]));

  # THE CLASS OF EACH UNIT, as measured: `nix/rust/unit-classes.json`,
  # written by `nix run .#rust-peaks -- --write` from the units' own
  # `metrics/peak-kib`. The one place a unit's class is said; refused, with
  # the command, when it was written against other slots or a headroom
  # other than these, or names a class that is none.
  classesFile = ./rust/unit-classes.json;
  classData =
    let
      d = lib.importJSON classesFile;
      bad = lib.filter (c: !(rank ? ${c})) (lib.unique (lib.attrValues d.units));
      redo = "run `nix run .#rust-peaks -- --write` after a build of the units";
    in
    # Format 2: the classes of the whole build's peak at the cores the class
    # builds with (format 1 was the largest process's, at any cores).
    if d.format or null != 2 then
      throw "nix/rust/unit-classes.json is in format ${toString (d.format or "none")}, not 2: ${redo}"
    else if d.slots != slotOf then
      throw "nix/rust/unit-classes.json was written for the slots ${builtins.toJSON d.slots}, and nix/build-sizing.nix says ${builtins.toJSON slotOf}: ${redo}"
    else if d.headroomPercent != numbers.headroomPercent then
      throw "nix/rust/unit-classes.json was written with ${toString d.headroomPercent}% of headroom, and nix/build-sizing.nix says ${toString numbers.headroomPercent}%: ${redo}"
    else if bad != [ ] then
      throw "nix/rust/unit-classes.json names classes nix/build-sizing.nix has not: ${lib.concatStringsSep ", " bad}"
    else
      d;

  # THE KINDS OF UNIT A PLAN SCHEDULES (`rust.unitIndex`): every one that
  # compiles or runs a compiler's output. Not the aggregates (link farms)
  # nor a layer (a cargo of its own, built before and alone).
  plannedKinds = [
    "deps"
    "libs"
    "scripts"
    "bins"
    "testBins"
    "testRuns"
    "clippy"
    "fmt"
  ];

  # THE CLASS OF EACH UNIT OF `units` (ids), and how it was found: `{ class;
  # how; }` by id, `how` one of
  #
  #   named    `classes` (the data, `id → class`) names it;
  #   carried  it does not, and the unit is a third-party one whose id
  #            differs from one it names only by the digest at its end — the
  #            same crate at the same version, target, mode and opt level;
  #            what the digest holds (features, profile, the dependencies'
  #            ids) changed, after a lock change its dependencies —, whose
  #            class (the heaviest, were there several) it takes;
  #   default  neither: `unclassed`, until it is measured.
  #
  # A class the data names that is none (a stale file read raw, as
  # `.#rust-peaks` reads it) is carried by nothing.
  classesOf =
    {
      units,
      classes ? classData.units,
    }:
    let
      # `<flavour>/deps/<crate>@<version>/<kind>:<target>/<mode>/o<n>/<digest>`
      # without its digest; `null` for an id that has none.
      stemOf =
        id:
        let
          m = builtins.match "([^/]+/deps/.+)/[0-9a-f]+" id;
        in
        if m == null then null else builtins.head m;
      heavier = a: b: if rank.${b} > rank.${a} then b else a;
      stemmed = lib.filter (id: stemOf id != null && rank ? ${classes.${id}}) (lib.attrNames classes);
      byStem = lib.mapAttrs (
        _: ids: lib.foldl' heavier (classes.${lib.head ids}) (map (i: classes.${i}) ids)
      ) (builtins.groupBy stemOf stemmed);
      found =
        id:
        let
          s = stemOf id;
        in
        if classes ? ${id} then
          {
            class = classes.${id};
            how = "named";
          }
        else if s != null && byStem ? ${s} then
          {
            class = byStem.${s};
            how = "carried";
          }
        else
          {
            class = numbers.unclassed;
            how = "default";
          };
    in
    lib.genAttrs units found;

  # WHAT A LINE OF A PLAN COSTS, in the words `sized_units` reads: the slot
  # in MiB, or a heavy unit's `alone:<MiB at one core>+<MiB a core more>`.
  costOf =
    class:
    if class == "heavy" then
      "alone:${toString numbers.shellMiB}+${toString numbers.besideMiB}"
    else
      toString slotOf.${class};

  # THE PLAN OF THE UNITS, for `sized_units`: of `units` (an index shaped as
  # `rust.unitIndex`, `id → { kind; drv; deps; … }`), the entries of `kinds`,
  # one line each,
  #
  #   <id> <class> <how> <cost> <.drv> <the lines it is built from>…
  #
  # (the grammar is `sized_units`'s, below), the lines numbered from 0, in
  # the order they start in when several are ready: the longest chain of
  # units above first (the critical path in units, which needs no time
  # measured), then by id — and the units of the kinds in `late` after all
  # the others, so that `lateFrom`, the number of the first of them, is
  # where `sized_units` starts them only once every other has ended: a test
  # that measures time (`late` holds the test runs) does not measure a
  # compiler beside it. A dependency the plan does not hold (a unit of
  # another kind, built before) is no line of it; a unit of another kind
  # built from a late one is refused, since it would wait for itself.
  #
  # `classes` is `id → class` (`classesOf`). The answer: `lines`, `text`
  # (the file), `lateFrom`, and the ids `heavy`, `carried` and `unnamed`.
  unitPlan =
    {
      units,
      kinds ? plannedKinds,
      classes ? classData.units,
      late ? [ "testRuns" ],
    }:
    let
      planned = lib.filterAttrs (_: e: lib.elem e.kind kinds) units;
      found = classesOf {
        units = lib.attrNames planned;
        inherit classes;
      };
      isLate = id: lib.elem planned.${id}.kind late;
      depsOf = id: lib.filter (d: planned ? ${d}) planned.${id}.deps;
      # Who is built from each unit, gathered once (`groupBy` is one pass;
      # an attribute set grown entry by entry would be quadratic).
      pairs = lib.concatLists (
        lib.mapAttrsToList (
          id: _:
          map (d: {
            inherit d id;
          }) (depsOf id)
        ) planned
      );
      users = builtins.groupBy (p: p.d) pairs;
      waitsForItself = lib.filter (p: isLate p.d && !(isLate p.id)) pairs;
      # The longest chain of units above each one, itself counted.
      height = lib.mapAttrs (
        id: _: 1 + lib.foldl' (m: p: lib.max m height.${p.id}) 0 (users.${id} or [ ])
      ) planned;
      before =
        a: b:
        let
          la = isLate a;
          lb = isLate b;
        in
        (!la && lb) || (la == lb && (height.${a} > height.${b} || (height.${a} == height.${b} && a < b)));
      order = lib.sort before (lib.attrNames planned);
      number = lib.listToAttrs (lib.imap0 (i: id: lib.nameValuePair id (toString i)) order);
      line =
        id:
        lib.concatStringsSep " " (
          [
            id
            found.${id}.class
            found.${id}.how
            (costOf found.${id}.class)
            planned.${id}.drv
          ]
          ++ map (d: number.${d}) (depsOf id)
        );
      lines = map line order;
      withHow = how: lib.filter (id: found.${id}.how == how) order;
    in
    if waitsForItself != [ ] then
      throw "unitPlan: ${(lib.head waitsForItself).id} is built from ${(lib.head waitsForItself).d}, a unit of a kind started last (${lib.concatStringsSep ", " late}): it would wait for itself"
    else
      {
        inherit lines;
        text = writeText "ronoctua-unit-plan" (lib.concatMapStrings (l: l + "\n") lines);
        lateFrom = lib.length (lib.filter (id: !(isLate id)) order);
        heavy = lib.filter (id: found.${id}.class == "heavy") order;
        carried = withHow "carried";
        unnamed = withHow "default";
      };

  # THE ARITHMETIC, as shell functions: sourced by the batteries and
  # `.#sized-build`, and run by `ronoctua-build-sizing` and
  # `ronoctua-build-units`, so there is one copy.
  #
  # `sizing_measure [meminfo] [cpus] [ceiling-bytes|infinity]` sets every
  # variable below; with no arguments it reads this machine. It refuses —
  # returns 1, with a sentence — a meminfo it cannot read and a CPU count
  # that is not one, never a plausible default.
  #
  # NO LOCALE MOVES A NUMBER HERE: the clock is `$EPOCHREALTIME` with every
  # character but its digits taken out (bash writes its fraction with the
  # locale's decimal mark, a comma in pt_BR, and `${EPOCHREALTIME/./}` left
  # `1760…,089…` for the arithmetic to read as a comma and an octal), and
  # every number a plan or an answer gives is read as digits — `[[:digit:]]`,
  # no leading zero — before any `$(( ))` sees it.
  functions = writeText "build-sizing.sh" ''
    # GENERATED from nix/build-sizing.nix. Sourced, never run.

    sizing_refuse() {
      echo "build sizing: $*" >&2
      return 1
    }

    # `sizing_cgroup_ceiling <cgroup fs> <cgroup>`: the smallest `memory.high`
    # or `memory.max` from that cgroup up to the root, in bytes, or nothing
    # (`max` everywhere) — the effective limit of what runs in it.
    sizing_cgroup_ceiling() {
      local fs=$1 own=$2 path limit best="" level
      path=$fs''${own%/}
      while :; do
        for level in memory.high memory.max; do
          limit=$(cat "$path/$level" 2>/dev/null || true)
          if [[ "$limit" =~ ^[[:digit:]]+$ ]] && { [ -z "$best" ] || [ "$limit" -lt "$best" ]; }; then
            best=$limit
          fi
        done
        [ "$path" != "$fs" ] || break
        path=''${path%/*}
        [ "''${#path}" -ge "''${#fs}" ] || break
      done
      echo "$best"
    }

    # THE CEILING OF WHERE THE BUILDS RUN: sets `ceiling_b` (bytes, or
    # nothing) and `sizing_ceiling_from` (what it is).
    sizing_read_ceiling() {
      local own
      if [ "$(id -u)" = 0 ] && [ -z "''${NIX_REMOTE:-}" ]; then
        # Root's own store: its builds are children of this process, in
        # this cgroup and under its parents' limits.
        sizing_ceiling_from="this process's cgroup"
        own=$(sed -n 's/^0::\(\/.*\)$/\1/p' /proc/self/cgroup 2>/dev/null || true)
        ceiling_b=""
        if [ -n "$own" ]; then
          ceiling_b=$(sizing_cgroup_ceiling /sys/fs/cgroup "$own")
        fi
      else
        # Anybody else hands the builds to the daemon. `infinity`, an empty
        # answer or no systemd (another distro, a container) is no ceiling.
        sizing_ceiling_from="nix-daemon's MemoryHigh"
        ceiling_b=$(systemctl show -p MemoryHigh --value nix-daemon.service 2>/dev/null || true)
        [[ "$ceiling_b" =~ ^[[:digit:]]+$ ]] || ceiling_b=""
      fi
    }

    sizing_measure() {
      local meminfo=''${1:-/proc/meminfo} given_cpus=''${2:-} given_ceiling=''${3:-}
      total_kb=$(awk '/^MemTotal:/ {print $2}' "$meminfo" 2>/dev/null || true)
      available_kb=$(awk '/^MemAvailable:/ {print $2}' "$meminfo" 2>/dev/null || true)
      if ! [[ "$total_kb" =~ ^[1-9][[:digit:]]*$ && "$available_kb" =~ ^(0|[1-9][[:digit:]]*)$ ]]; then
        sizing_refuse "$meminfo says no MemTotal and MemAvailable in kB: nothing to size by"
        return 1
      fi
      if [ -n "$given_cpus" ]; then
        cpus=$given_cpus
      else
        cpus=$(nproc)
      fi
      if ! [[ "$cpus" =~ ^[1-9][[:digit:]]*$ ]]; then
        sizing_refuse "$cpus is not a count of processors"
        return 1
      fi
      floor_kb=$(( total_kb * ${toString numbers.floorPercent} / 100 ))
      reserve_kb=$(( ${toString numbers.reserveMiB} * 1024 ))
      peak_kb=$(( ${toString numbers.peakMiB} * 1024 ))
      client_kb=$(( ${toString numbers.clientMiB} * 1024 ))

      # NEVER PAST THE CEILING, read once: every budget below follows it.
      sizing_ceiling_from=""
      case "$given_ceiling" in
        "") sizing_read_ceiling ;;
        infinity) ceiling_b=""; sizing_ceiling_from="given" ;;
        *)
          if ! [[ "$given_ceiling" =~ ^[1-9][[:digit:]]*$ ]]; then
            sizing_refuse "$given_ceiling is not a ceiling in bytes, nor infinity"
            return 1
          fi
          ceiling_b=$given_ceiling
          sizing_ceiling_from="given"
          ;;
      esac
      ceiling_kb=""
      if [ -n "$ceiling_b" ]; then
        ceiling_kb=$(( ceiling_b / 1024 - reserve_kb ))
        if [ "$ceiling_kb" -lt 0 ]; then
          ceiling_kb=0
        fi
      fi

      budget_kb=$(sizing_under_ceiling $(( available_kb - floor_kb - reserve_kb )))
      if [ "$budget_kb" -lt "$peak_kb" ]; then
        slots=1
      else
        slots=$(( budget_kb / peak_kb ))
      fi
      [ "$slots" -le "$cpus" ] || slots=$cpus

      # EVERYTHING THAT IS NO UNIT: at most four at once, the slots split
      # among them. The units are planned by their own lines
      # (`sized_units`), which carry their numbers.
      jobs=$slots
      [ "$jobs" -le ${toString numbers.jobsCeiling} ] || jobs=${toString numbers.jobsCeiling}
      cores=$(( slots / jobs ))
      [ "$cores" -ge 1 ] || cores=1
      return 0
    }

    # `sizing_under_ceiling <kB>`: the smaller of what is free and the ceiling
    # (`sizing_measure` read it). A negative budget is no budget.
    sizing_under_ceiling() {
      local kb=$1
      if [ -n "''${ceiling_kb:-}" ] && [ "$ceiling_kb" -lt "$kb" ]; then
        kb=$ceiling_kb
      fi
      if [ "$kb" -lt 0 ]; then
        kb=0
      fi
      echo "$kb"
    }

    # WHAT WAS DECIDED, said. MiB and not GiB: the division truncates, and a
    # floor of 1466 MiB shown as "1 GiB" is a number that argues with the
    # arithmetic beside it.
    sizing_say() {
      printf 'memory: %s MiB in total, %s MiB available; %s processors\n' \
        "$(( total_kb / 1024 ))" "$(( available_kb / 1024 ))" "$cpus"
      if [ -n "$ceiling_kb" ]; then
        printf 'ceiling (%s): %s MiB, and every budget follows it\n' \
          "$sizing_ceiling_from" "$(( (ceiling_kb + reserve_kb) / 1024 ))"
      fi
      printf 'earlyoom floor: %s MiB;  reserve: %s MiB;  the budget: %s MiB\n' \
        "$(( floor_kb / 1024 ))" "$(( reserve_kb / 1024 ))" "$(( budget_kb / 1024 ))"
      printf '%s compilations fit at once -> what is no unit: --max-jobs %s --cores %s (%s MiB each);  each unit by the slot its plan gives it, and %s MiB for its own nix\n' \
        "$slots" "$jobs" "$cores" "$(( peak_kb / 1024 ))" "$(( client_kb / 1024 ))"
    }

    # A SIGNAL STOPS THE BUILDS THIS SHELL STARTED, and waits for them,
    # before the caller's own clean-up (its `EXIT` trap) runs: killed alone,
    # the scheduler left its builds running, orphaned, and its clean-up
    # removed the folder that held their out-links from under them.
    # `sizing_stop_on_signals` arms it for TERM, HUP and INT; the shell then
    # ends as a shell killed by that signal would (128 and its number).
    sizing_on_signal() {
      local name=$1 number=$2 pids
      trap - TERM HUP INT
      pids=$(jobs -pr)
      if [ -n "$pids" ]; then
        echo "build sizing: $name: stopping the $(wc -w <<< "$pids") build(s) still running, and waiting for them" >&2
        # shellcheck disable=SC2086
        kill -TERM $pids 2>/dev/null || true
        # shellcheck disable=SC2086
        wait $pids 2>/dev/null || true
      fi
      exit $(( 128 + number ))
    }
    sizing_stop_on_signals() {
      trap 'sizing_on_signal TERM 15' TERM
      trap 'sizing_on_signal HUP 1' HUP
      trap 'sizing_on_signal INT 2' INT
    }

    # `sizing_will_build`: what Nix's dry run (its standard error, on standard
    # input) says will be built — the `.drv`s under "will be built:", one a
    # line. That is Nix's own plan: a unit whose consumer the store already
    # holds is not in it, though it is in the `.drv` closure. Returns 1 for
    # words it does not read (a count that is not the paths under it, a path
    # there that is not a `.drv`, a store path under no heading it knows),
    # never a shorter list than Nix said. The same rules as
    # `ronoctua_machine::dry::to_build`.
    sizing_will_build() {
      awk '
        function close_heading() {
          if (built && listed != said) bad = 1
          built = 0
        }
        /^[[:space:]]+\/nix\/store\// {
          if (built) {
            if (NF != 1 || $1 !~ /\.drv$/) bad = 1
            listed++
            print $1
          } else if (!other) {
            bad = 1
          }
          next
        }
        {
          close_heading()
          other = 0
          line = $0
          sub(/[[:space:]]+$/, "", line)
          if (line ~ /will be built:$/) {
            built = 1
            listed = 0
            if ($1 == "this") said = 1
            else if ($1 == "these" && $2 ~ /^[0-9]+$/) said = $2 + 0
            else said = -1
          } else if (line ~ /will be fetched/ || line ~ /know how to build/) {
            other = 1
          }
        }
        END { close_heading(); exit bad }
      '
    }

    # `sizing_needing <missing> <candidates>`: of the derivations the file
    # <candidates> lists, one a line, every one that needs one the file
    # <missing> lists — that one itself, or anything built from it at any
    # depth —, by the store's own references (`nix-store --query
    # --referrers-closure`, a question of its database that builds
    # nothing; `$sizing_nix_store`, `nix-store` if unset). Returns 1 when the
    # store does not answer. Its answer is written beside <candidates>
    # (`.referrers`), and the question runs in the background, waited for:
    # a signal is handled at once (`sizing_on_signal` stops it), where a
    # command in the foreground has the trap wait for it to end.
    sizing_needing() {
      local store=''${sizing_nix_store:-nix-store} d scratch="$2.referrers"
      local -A needs=()
      local -a missing=()
      while IFS= read -r d || [ -n "$d" ]; do
        if [ -n "$d" ]; then missing+=("$d"); fi
      done < "$1"
      [ "''${#missing[@]}" -gt 0 ] || return 0
      "$store" --query --referrers-closure "''${missing[@]}" > "$scratch" &
      wait "$!" || return 1
      while IFS= read -r d; do
        if [ -n "$d" ]; then needs[$d]=1; fi
      done < "$scratch"
      rm -f "$scratch"
      while IFS= read -r d || [ -n "$d" ]; do
        if [ -n "$d" ] && [ -n "''${needs[$d]:-}" ]; then echo "$d"; fi
      done < "$2"
    }

    # `sizing_below <will> <from>`: of the derivations the file <will> lists,
    # one a line, every one that a derivation the file <from> lists is built
    # from — at any depth, through derivations <will> lists only —, said
    # once each; nothing <from> lists. <will> is what a dry run said will be
    # built, so this is what the dry run of <from> would list beside them: a
    # derivation the store holds, or one that is fetched, is in no list and
    # no step down. Asked of the store's references, level by level
    # (`nix-store --query --references` of what was found last; a question
    # of its database that builds nothing and asks no substituter): of what
    # will be built and what it is built from directly, never the whole
    # closure. Returns 1 when the store does not answer. The answers are
    # written beside <from> (`.references`), each question in the
    # background, waited for (see `sizing_needing`).
    sizing_below() {
      local store=''${sizing_nix_store:-nix-store} d scratch="$2.references"
      local -A will=() seen=()
      local -a frontier=() next=()
      while IFS= read -r d || [ -n "$d" ]; do
        if [ -n "$d" ]; then will[$d]=1; fi
      done < "$1"
      while IFS= read -r d || [ -n "$d" ]; do
        if [ -n "$d" ] && [ -z "''${seen[$d]:-}" ]; then
          seen[$d]=1
          frontier+=("$d")
        fi
      done < "$2"
      while [ "''${#frontier[@]}" -gt 0 ]; do
        "$store" --query --references "''${frontier[@]}" > "$scratch" &
        wait "$!" || return 1
        # Of a program's hundreds of references, the few <will> lists, by
        # `grep` and not line by line in the shell.
        grep -xF -f "$1" "$scratch" > "$scratch.listed" || true
        next=()
        while IFS= read -r d; do
          if [ -n "''${will[$d]:-}" ] && [ -z "''${seen[$d]:-}" ]; then
            seen[$d]=1
            next+=("$d")
            echo "$d"
          fi
        done < "$scratch.listed"
        frontier=("''${next[@]}")
      done
      rm -f "$scratch" "$scratch.listed"
    }

    # `sizing_scan_plan <plan> <will>`: the plan (`sized_units`'s grammar,
    # below) read whole by `awk`, every line held to the grammar — a loop of
    # the shell over every word of every line, some forty thousand numbers
    # in the distro's, cost a second and a half before every build. If all
    # of it reads: `U <line number, from 0> <the line>` for each line whose
    # `.drv` the file <will> lists, `X <.drv>` for each derivation <will>
    # lists that no line is, and `L <how many lines>`. A line it does not
    # read is `R <the sentence>`, and it answers 2.
    sizing_scan_plan() {
      awk -v will="$2" -v plan="$1" '
        function refuse(why) {
          if (!bad) printf "R line %d of %s%s\n", FNR, plan, why
          bad = 1
          exit 2
        }
        BEGIN {
          deepest = -1
          while ((getline d < will) > 0) if (d != "") listed[d] = 1
        }
        {
          if (NF < 5) refuse(" is no unit: <id> <class> <how> <cost> <.drv> <the lines it is built from>…")
          if ($2 !~ /^[[:alnum:]][[:alnum:]_-]*$/) refuse(": " $2 " is no class\047s name")
          # How the class was found is said, never read: any name, so that
          # a word a newer distro added is no refusal (which would revert
          # every update of a machine running this program).
          if ($3 !~ /^[[:alnum:]][[:alnum:]_-]*$/) refuse(": " $3 " is not a name of how a class is found")
          if ($4 !~ /^[1-9][[:digit:]]*$/ && $4 !~ /^alone:[1-9][[:digit:]]*\+[1-9][[:digit:]]*$/)
            refuse(": " $4 " is no cost (<MiB>, or alone:<MiB at one core>+<MiB a core more>)")
          if ($5 !~ /^\/nix\/store\/.*\.drv$/) refuse(": " $5 " is no derivation")
          for (i = 6; i <= NF; i++) {
            if ($i !~ /^(0|[1-9][[:digit:]]*)$/) refuse(": " $i " is not a line\047s number")
            if ($i + 0 > deepest) { deepest = $i + 0; deepest_at = FNR }
          }
          lines = FNR
          planned[$5] = 1
          if ($5 in listed) printf "U %d %s\n", FNR - 1, $0
        }
        END {
          if (bad) exit 2
          if (deepest >= lines) {
            printf "R line %d of %s: %d is no line of it (it has %d)\n", deepest_at, plan, deepest, lines
            exit 2
          }
          for (d in listed) if (!(d in planned)) print "X " d
          print "L " lines + 0
        }
      ' "$1"
    }

    # `sized_units <plan> <will> <held>`: the units of the plan that Nix
    # will build — <will> is the caller's dry run, whole: every `.drv` it
    # said will be built, one a line, the units of the plan and what is no
    # unit; a unit missing there the store holds already — each its own
    # `nix build` (`$sizing_nix`, `nix` if unset, with `sized_flags`), its
    # outputs held under <held> for as long as the caller keeps that folder.
    # With `sizing_will_units_only=1`, <will> lists units and nothing else
    # (no dry run answered the caller), and Nix is asked what they are
    # built from: their own dry run.
    #
    # THE PLAN (`unitPlan`), one unit a line:
    #
    #   <id> <class> <how> <cost> <.drv> <the lines it is built from>…
    #
    # <class> is a name, said and never read: the cost is all the arithmetic
    # needs, so a plan from a newer distro — a class this program never heard
    # of, a slot resized — is built by its own numbers. <how> is how the
    # class was found (`named`, `carried`, `default`; `classesOf`), a name
    # too: the three are counted and said, and a word a newer distro added
    # is built as any other line. <cost> is `<MiB>`, a slot built at one
    # core beside others, or
    # `alone:<MiB>+<MiB>`, a unit built alone with the cores the budget
    # leaves: the first number at one core, the second for each core more.
    # The lines it is built from are numbers of lines, from 0. A plan in any
    # other words is refused before anything builds, never guessed at.
    #
    # FIRST, what the units are built from that is no unit — what <will>
    # lists that no line of the plan is, below a unit to build
    # (`sizing_below`) —, sized as what is no unit: never inside a unit's
    # build, at its one core and in its slot. Then a unit
    # starts once every unit of the plan it is built from has been built, in
    # the plan's order, when its slot and its own client fit what is left of
    # the budget beside what runs, and never more at once than processors;
    # one alone always starts, whatever it costs. A unit that is `alone`
    # starts when nothing else can — nothing runs and no other unit is ready
    # — with the cores its cost leaves room for, and nothing starts beside
    # it. Every other unit gets one core: a unit is one `rustc`, and the many
    # small ones are what the graph is. The lines numbered from
    # `$sizing_late_from` on start only once every line before it has ended
    # (built, failed or not to be built).
    #
    # A UNIT THAT FAILS is never built again, and neither is anything built
    # from it: with `sizing_keep_going=1` those are not built (one line says
    # which, and why) and every other unit still is; without it nothing
    # starts after the failure. So with what the units are built from that
    # is no unit: built with `--keep-going` when it is on, so one that fails
    # does not stop the others, and only the units that need one that did
    # not build are left out. What was not built — a unit that failed or
    # never started, and what they are built from that did not build — is
    # written to <held>/not-built, one `.drv` a line, for the caller's later
    # builds to leave out (`sizing_needing`).
    #
    # ITS BUILDS SAY LINES, NEVER A BAR: several `nix build` drawing their
    # progress bars on one terminal overwrote each other and glued this
    # scheduler's own lines to theirs. Each build's standard error goes
    # through a pipe, where Nix writes plain lines — with `-L`, each line of
    # a build's log after its name —, and without `-L` it is `--quiet` too:
    # a unit says nothing but its failure (Nix's error and the log's tail),
    # beside the scheduler's `->` and `<-`.
    #
    # Every question it asks the store or Nix runs in the background and is
    # waited for (see `sizing_needing`): a signal stops it at once.
    #
    # Returns 0 when every unit was built; 1 when one was not; 2 for a plan
    # it does not read, or a store or a dry run that does not answer, before
    # any build.
    sized_units() {
      local plan=$1 willfile=$2 held=$3
      local -A running=() groups=() extra=()
      local -a id=() cls=() how=() kb=() per=() solo=() drv=() deps=() left=() users=() ready=()
      local -a began=() weight=() cores_of=() line_at=() late=() over=() ok=() fin=()
      local -a words more needed stars unit_flags prereq_flags
      local n=0 lineno=0 i j c k d g w rc pid t0 now at
      local used=0 nrun=0 alone=0 stop=0 early=0 carried=0 unnamed=0 unnamed_as=""
      local built=0 failed=0 most=0 widest=0 ms lightest=""
      local nixcmd=''${sizing_nix:-nix} late_from=''${sizing_late_from:-}
      # What every build is also handed: a caller that set nothing hands
      # nothing.
      if ! declare -p sized_flags > /dev/null 2>&1; then sized_flags=(); fi
      unit_flags=("''${sized_flags[@]}" --quiet)
      for w in "''${sized_flags[@]}"; do
        case "$w" in
          -L | --print-build-logs) unit_flags=("''${sized_flags[@]}") ;;
        esac
      done
      mkdir -p "$held"
      # NOTHING IS BUILT until it is: a refusal leaves every derivation the
      # dry run listed written down as not built.
      cp "$willfile" "$held/not-built"
      if [ -n "$late_from" ] && ! [[ "$late_from" =~ ^(0|[1-9][[:digit:]]*)$ ]]; then
        sizing_refuse "$late_from is not a line's number (sizing_late_from)" || return 2
      fi
      # THE PLAN, READ WHOLE before anything builds (`sizing_scan_plan`): a
      # line it does not read is a refusal, never a unit guessed at, and of
      # the rest only the lines of what Nix will build are read here.
      # (`|| return 2`: under `errexit` the refusal's own 1 would end the
      # caller.)
      if ! sizing_scan_plan "$plan" "$willfile" > "$held/plan-read"; then
        sizing_refuse "$(sed -n 's/^R //p' "$held/plan-read")" || return 2
      fi
      while read -r -a words; do
        case "''${words[0]}" in
          L) lineno=''${words[1]} ;;
          X) extra[''${words[1]}]=1 ;;
          U)
            at=''${words[1]}
            words=("''${words[@]:2}")
            if [[ "''${words[3]}" =~ ^alone:([1-9][[:digit:]]*)\+([1-9][[:digit:]]*)$ ]]; then
              c=''${BASH_REMATCH[1]} k=''${BASH_REMATCH[2]}
            else
              c=''${words[3]} k=0
            fi
            id[n]=''${words[0]}
            cls[n]=''${words[1]}
            how[n]=''${words[2]}
            drv[n]=''${words[4]}
            deps[n]="''${words[*]:5}"
            kb[n]=$(( c * 1024 ))
            if [ "$k" -eq 0 ]; then
              per[n]=0
              cores_of[n]=1
              weight[n]=$(( kb[n] + client_kb ))
              if [ -z "$lightest" ] || [ "''${weight[n]}" -lt "$lightest" ]; then lightest=''${weight[n]}; fi
            else
              # ALONE: its compiler at one core, and what each core more
              # adds, with the cores the budget leaves.
              solo[n]=1
              per[n]=$(( k * 1024 ))
              if [ "$budget_kb" -le "''${kb[n]}" ]; then
                cores_of[n]=1
              else
                cores_of[n]=$(( 1 + (budget_kb - kb[n]) / per[n] ))
              fi
              [ "''${cores_of[n]}" -le "$cpus" ] || cores_of[n]=$cpus
              weight[n]=$(( kb[n] + (cores_of[n] - 1) * per[n] ))
            fi
            if [ -n "$late_from" ] && [ "$at" -ge "$late_from" ]; then
              late[n]=1
            else
              early=$(( early + 1 ))
            fi
            line_at[at]=$n
            n=$(( n + 1 ))
            ;;
        esac
      done < "$held/plan-read"
      # WHAT EACH WAITS FOR: only what Nix will build; the rest is there.
      for (( i = 0; i < n; i++ )); do
        left[i]=0
        read -r -a more <<< "''${deps[i]}"
        for d in "''${more[@]}"; do
          j=''${line_at[d]:-}
          if [ -n "$j" ]; then
            left[i]=$(( left[i] + 1 ))
            users[j]+=" $i"
          fi
        done
        if [ "''${left[i]}" -eq 0 ]; then ready[i]=1; fi
        if [ -n "''${solo[i]:-}" ]; then
          g="''${cls[i]}, alone with ''${cores_of[i]} cores ($(( kb[i] / 1024 )) MiB and $(( per[i] / 1024 )) MiB a core more)"
        else
          g="''${cls[i]}, $(( kb[i] / 1024 )) MiB each"
        fi
        groups[$g]=$(( ''${groups[$g]:-0} + 1 ))
        case "''${how[i]}" in
          carried) carried=$(( carried + 1 )) ;;
          default)
            unnamed=$(( unnamed + 1 ))
            unnamed_as="''${cls[i]} ($(( kb[i] / 1024 )) MiB)"
            ;;
        esac
      done
      [ -n "$lightest" ] || lightest=$(( budget_kb + 1 ))
      printf '%s\n' "''${drv[@]}" > "$held/not-built"
      echo "-- $n units to build (of $lineno in the plan), as many at once as their slots and $(( client_kb / 1024 )) MiB each for its own nix fit $(( budget_kb / 1024 )) MiB:"
      while IFS= read -r g; do
        echo "     $g"
      done < <(for g in "''${!groups[@]}"; do printf '%s: %s\n' "''${groups[$g]}" "$g"; done | sort -t: -k1,1nr)
      if [ "$unnamed" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "-- $unnamed of them the class data does not name, and they are built as $unnamed_as until it does: in the distro's checkout, after a build of them, nix run .#rust-peaks -- --write measures them"
      fi
      if [ "$carried" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "-- $carried of them carry the class of the unit a lock change renamed (the same crate and version, on other dependencies), until nix run .#rust-peaks -- --write measures them"
      fi
      if [ "$n" -eq 0 ]; then
        : > "$held/not-built"
        return 0
      fi

      # WHAT THEY ARE BUILT FROM THAT IS NO UNIT, built first, sized as what
      # is no unit. <will> is the caller's dry run, whole: what of it is no
      # line of the plan and is below a unit to build (`sizing_below`, the
      # store's references) is what the units' own builds would build. No
      # second dry run: it would ask Nix — and every substituter, of every
      # unit — again what the caller's had answered. When <will> lists
      # nothing beside the units, there is nothing to ask.
      printf '%s\n' "''${drv[@]}" > "$held/units-to-build"
      needed=()
      : > "$held/needed-will"
      if [ "''${sizing_will_units_only:-0}" = 1 ]; then
        # NO DRY RUN ANSWERED THE CALLER: <will> is units only, and Nix is
        # asked what they are built from — their own dry run.
        stars=()
        for (( i = 0; i < n; i++ )); do stars+=("''${drv[i]}^*"); done
        "$nixcmd" build --dry-run "''${sized_flags[@]}" "''${stars[@]}" > /dev/null 2> "$held/units-dry" &
        if ! wait "$!" || ! sizing_will_build < "$held/units-dry" > "$held/needed-will"; then
          tail -n 5 "$held/units-dry" >&2
          printf '%s\n' "''${drv[@]}" > "$held/not-built"
          sizing_refuse "the dry run of the $n units did not say what their builds build, in words this reads: nothing is built" || return 2
        fi
      elif [ "''${#extra[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
        if ! sizing_below "$willfile" "$held/units-to-build" > "$held/needed-will"; then
          printf '%s\n' "''${drv[@]}" > "$held/not-built"
          sizing_refuse "the store did not say what the $n units are built from: nothing is built" || return 2
        fi
      fi
      # Of what was found, what no line of the plan is.
      while IFS= read -r d; do
        needed+=("$d")
      done < <(awk 'NR == FNR { p[$5] = 1; next } $1 != "" && !($1 in p) { print $1 }' "$plan" "$held/needed-will")
      : > "$held/needed-missing"
      if [ "''${#needed[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "-- first, what they are built from that is no unit: ''${#needed[@]} derivation(s), sized as what is no unit (--max-jobs $jobs --cores $cores)"
        # With `--keep-going` when it is on: without it Nix stops every
        # other build at the first failure, and every unit that needs one
        # of those would be left out, though nothing it needs failed.
        prereq_flags=("''${unit_flags[@]}")
        if [ "''${sizing_keep_going:-0}" = 1 ]; then prereq_flags+=(--keep-going); fi
        "$nixcmd" build --out-link "$held/needed" "''${prereq_flags[@]}" \
          --max-jobs "$jobs" --cores "$cores" "''${needed[@]/%/^*}" 2> >(cat >&2) &
        if wait "$!"; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi
        if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
          if [ "''${sizing_keep_going:-0}" != 1 ]; then
            echo "-- what they are built from that is no unit did not build (named above): no unit is built"
            printf '%s\n' "''${drv[@]}" "''${needed[@]}" > "$held/not-built"
            return 1
          fi
          # WHAT IS STILL MISSING of it, and every unit that needs it: never
          # handed a build, which would build it again in a unit's slot.
          "$nixcmd" build --dry-run "''${sized_flags[@]}" "''${needed[@]/%/^*}" > /dev/null 2> "$held/needed-dry" &
          if wait "$!" \
              && sizing_will_build < "$held/needed-dry" > "$held/needed-missing" \
              && sizing_needing "$held/needed-missing" "$held/units-to-build" > "$held/needed-blocked"; then
            :
          else
            echo "-- the store did not say which units need what did not build: no unit is built"
            printf '%s\n' "''${drv[@]}" "''${needed[@]}" > "$held/not-built"
            return 1
          fi
          local -A blocked=()
          while IFS= read -r d; do
            if [ -n "$d" ]; then blocked[$d]=1; fi
          done < "$held/needed-blocked"
          k=0 g=""
          for (( i = 0; i < n; i++ )); do
            if [ -n "''${blocked[''${drv[i]}]:-}" ]; then
              over[i]=1
              unset "ready[i]"
              if [ -z "''${late[i]:-}" ]; then early=$(( early - 1 )); fi
              k=$(( k + 1 ))
              if [ "$k" -le 5 ]; then g+="''${g:+, }''${id[i]}"; fi
            fi
          done
          if [ "$k" -gt 5 ]; then g+=" and $(( k - 5 )) more"; fi
          if [ "$k" -gt 0 ]; then
            echo "-- $k unit(s) are not built, because what they are built from did not build: $g"
          fi
        fi
      fi

      # Every unit built from <i>, at any depth, still to build: not built,
      # and said once, with <why>.
      sized_drop_users() {
        local from=$1 why=$2 x y count=0 names=""
        local -a queue=("$1") next
        while [ "''${#queue[@]}" -gt 0 ]; do
          x=''${queue[0]}
          queue=("''${queue[@]:1}")
          read -r -a next <<< "''${users[x]:-}"
          for y in "''${next[@]}"; do
            if [ -z "''${over[y]:-}" ] && [ -z "''${fin[y]:-}" ]; then
              over[y]=1
              unset "ready[y]"
              if [ -z "''${late[y]:-}" ]; then early=$(( early - 1 )); fi
              count=$(( count + 1 ))
              if [ "$count" -le 5 ]; then names+="''${names:+, }''${id[y]}"; fi
              queue+=("$y")
            fi
          done
        done
        if [ "$count" -gt 5 ]; then names+=" and $(( count - 5 )) more"; fi
        if [ "$count" -gt 0 ]; then
          echo "-- $count unit(s) built from ''${id[from]} are not built, $why: $names"
        fi
      }

      t0=''${EPOCHREALTIME//[!0123456789]/}
      sized_unit_start() {
        local i=$1
        "$nixcmd" build --out-link "$held/unit-$i" "''${unit_flags[@]}" \
          --max-jobs 1 --cores "''${cores_of[i]}" "''${drv[i]}^*" 2> >(cat >&2) &
        running[$!]=$i
        unset "ready[i]"
        began[i]=''${EPOCHREALTIME//[!0123456789]/}
        used=$(( used + weight[i] ))
        nrun=$(( nrun + 1 ))
        [ "$nrun" -le "$most" ] || most=$nrun
        [ "$used" -le "$widest" ] || widest=$used
        ms=$(( (began[i] - t0) / 1000 ))
        if [ -n "''${solo[i]:-}" ]; then
          printf -- '[%d.%d s] -> %s (%s, alone with %s cores, %s of %s MiB)\n' \
            "$(( ms / 1000 ))" "$(( ms % 1000 / 100 ))" "''${id[i]}" "''${cls[i]}" "''${cores_of[i]}" \
            "$(( used / 1024 ))" "$(( budget_kb / 1024 ))"
        else
          printf -- '[%d.%d s] -> %s (%s, %s running, %s of %s MiB)\n' \
            "$(( ms / 1000 ))" "$(( ms % 1000 / 100 ))" "''${id[i]}" "''${cls[i]}" "$nrun" \
            "$(( used / 1024 ))" "$(( budget_kb / 1024 ))"
        fi
      }
      while :; do
        if [ "$stop" -eq 0 ] && [ "$alone" -eq 0 ]; then
          for i in "''${!ready[@]}"; do
            if [ "$nrun" -gt 0 ] && { [ "$nrun" -ge "$cpus" ] || [ $(( used + lightest )) -gt "$budget_kb" ]; }; then
              break
            fi
            [ -z "''${solo[i]:-}" ] || continue
            if [ -n "''${late[i]:-}" ] && [ "$early" -gt 0 ]; then continue; fi
            if [ "$nrun" -eq 0 ] || { [ $(( used + weight[i] )) -le "$budget_kb" ] && [ "$nrun" -lt "$cpus" ]; }; then
              sized_unit_start "$i"
            fi
          done
          if [ "$nrun" -eq 0 ]; then
            for i in "''${!ready[@]}"; do
              [ -n "''${solo[i]:-}" ] || continue
              if [ -n "''${late[i]:-}" ] && [ "$early" -gt 0 ]; then continue; fi
              alone=1
              sized_unit_start "$i"
              break
            done
          fi
        fi
        [ "$nrun" -gt 0 ] || break
        if wait -n -p pid "''${!running[@]}"; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi
        i=''${running[$pid]}
        unset "running[$pid]"
        nrun=$(( nrun - 1 ))
        used=$(( used - weight[i] ))
        if [ -n "''${solo[i]:-}" ]; then alone=0; fi
        fin[i]=1
        if [ -z "''${late[i]:-}" ]; then early=$(( early - 1 )); fi
        now=''${EPOCHREALTIME//[!0123456789]/}
        ms=$(( (now - began[i]) / 1000 ))
        if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
          built=$(( built + 1 ))
          ok[i]=1
          printf -- '<- %s: built in %d.%d s\n' "''${id[i]}" "$(( ms / 1000 ))" "$(( ms % 1000 / 100 ))"
          read -r -a more <<< "''${users[i]:-}"
          for j in "''${more[@]}"; do
            left[j]=$(( left[j] - 1 ))
            if [ "''${left[j]}" -eq 0 ] && [ -z "''${over[j]:-}" ]; then ready[j]=1; fi
          done
        else
          failed=$(( failed + 1 ))
          printf -- '<- %s: FAILED after %d.%d s\n' "''${id[i]}" "$(( ms / 1000 ))" "$(( ms % 1000 / 100 ))"
          if [ "''${sizing_keep_going:-0}" = 1 ]; then
            sized_drop_users "$i" "because it failed"
          else
            stop=1
          fi
        fi
      done
      {
        for (( i = 0; i < n; i++ )); do
          if [ -z "''${ok[i]:-}" ]; then echo "''${drv[i]}"; fi
        done
        # And what they are built from that did not build: a later build
        # handed it would build it again.
        cat "$held/needed-missing"
      } > "$held/not-built"
      now=''${EPOCHREALTIME//[!0123456789]/}
      ms=$(( (now - t0) / 1000 ))
      echo "-- $built of $n units built in $(( ms / 1000 )) s; at most $most at once, at most $(( widest / 1024 )) MiB planned at once (the budget: $(( budget_kb / 1024 )) MiB)"
      if [ "$failed" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "-- $failed unit(s) FAILED (named above)"
      fi
      if [ $(( n - built - failed )) -gt 0 ] && [ "$stop" -eq 1 ]; then
        echo "-- $(( n - built - failed )) unit(s) not built: nothing starts after a failure without --keep-going"
      elif [ $(( n - built - failed )) -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "-- $(( n - built - failed )) unit(s) not built: built from what failed or did not build (named above)"
      fi
      [ "$built" -eq "$n" ]
    }
  '';

  # THE PROGRAM: the same arithmetic, printed as `name=value` lines for a
  # program to read — `ronoctua-machine apply` sizes its system's build with
  # it — and, with `--explain`, said on standard error. `--meminfo`, `--cpus`
  # and `--ceiling` measure another machine (a check plants one); without
  # them it measures this one.
  program = writeShellApplication {
    name = "ronoctua-build-sizing";
    # `sizing_measure` sets the numbers, where shellcheck cannot follow.
    excludeShellChecks = [ "SC2154" ];
    runtimeInputs = [
      coreutils
      gawk
      gnugrep
      gnused
    ];
    text = ''
      # shellcheck source=/dev/null
      . ${functions}
      meminfo="" given_cpus="" given_ceiling="" explain=0
      needs() {
        if [ "$1" -lt 2 ]; then
          echo "ronoctua-build-sizing: $2 needs a value" >&2
          exit 2
        fi
      }
      while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
        case "$1" in
          --meminfo) needs $# "$1"; meminfo=$2; shift ;;
          --cpus) needs $# "$1"; given_cpus=$2; shift ;;
          --ceiling) needs $# "$1"; given_ceiling=$2; shift ;;
          --explain) explain=1 ;;
          -h | --help)
            echo "usage: ronoctua-build-sizing [--explain] [--meminfo FILE] [--cpus N] [--ceiling BYTES|infinity]"
            echo "prints how many compilations fit at once here, as name=value lines"
            exit 0
            ;;
          *) echo "ronoctua-build-sizing: unknown argument $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        shift
      done
      sizing_measure "$meminfo" "$given_cpus" "$given_ceiling" || exit 1
      if [ "$explain" = 1 ]; then
        sizing_say >&2
      fi
      for name in total_kb available_kb cpus floor_kb reserve_kb peak_kb client_kb \
                  ceiling_kb budget_kb slots jobs cores; do
        printf '%s=%s\n' "$name" "''${!name}"
      done
    '';
  };

  # THE UNITS OF A PLAN, BUILT: `sized_units` as a program, for what does not
  # source the functions — `ronoctua-machine apply` hands it the plan its
  # distro describes and what its dry run said will be built (all of it:
  # the units, and what is no unit, of which it builds first what the units
  # are built from), or, with `--units-only`, every unit when no dry run
  # answered. It sizes
  # itself (the same `sizing_measure`; `--meminfo`, `--cpus` and `--ceiling`
  # as `ronoctua-build-sizing` takes them), says how, and answers 0 only
  # when every unit was built. The plan carries every number of the units:
  # this program holds no class of its own (see "The plan carries its own
  # numbers" above).
  unitsProgram = writeShellApplication {
    name = "ronoctua-build-units";
    # `sizing_measure` sets the numbers and `sized_units` reads what is set
    # here, where shellcheck cannot follow.
    excludeShellChecks = [
      "SC2154"
      "SC2034"
    ];
    runtimeInputs = [
      coreutils
      gawk
      gnugrep
      gnused
    ];
    text = ''
      # shellcheck source=/dev/null
      . ${functions}
      sizing_stop_on_signals
      usage() {
        echo "usage: ronoctua-build-units [--nix PATH] [--keep-going] [--units-only] [--meminfo FILE] [--cpus N] [--ceiling BYTES|infinity] PLAN WILL HELD [-- NIX-ARGUMENT…]"
        echo "builds the units of PLAN that WILL (a dry run's derivations, one a line) lists, each its own"
        echo "nix build, as many at once as the slots their lines give fit the memory; their outputs held in HELD"
        echo "--units-only: WILL lists units only, and no dry run said what they are built from: ask Nix"
      }
      needs() {
        if [ "$1" -lt 2 ]; then
          echo "ronoctua-build-units: $2 needs a value" >&2
          exit 2
        fi
      }
      sizing_nix=nix sized_flags=() given=() meminfo="" given_cpus="" given_ceiling=""
      while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
        case "$1" in
          --nix) needs $# "$1"; sizing_nix=$2; shift ;;
          --keep-going) sizing_keep_going=1 ;;
          --units-only) sizing_will_units_only=1 ;;
          --meminfo) needs $# "$1"; meminfo=$2; shift ;;
          --cpus) needs $# "$1"; given_cpus=$2; shift ;;
          --ceiling) needs $# "$1"; given_ceiling=$2; shift ;;
          -h | --help) usage; exit 0 ;;
          --) shift; sized_flags+=("$@"); break ;;
          -*) echo "ronoctua-build-units: $1 is not an option this takes (see --help)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
          *) given+=("$1") ;;
        esac
        shift
      done
      [ "''${#given[@]}" -eq 3 ] || { usage >&2; exit 2; }
      # The store's own questions go to the `nix-store` beside that `nix`.
      if [[ "$sizing_nix" == */* ]]; then
        sizing_nix_store=''${sizing_nix%/*}/nix-store
      fi
      sizing_measure "$meminfo" "$given_cpus" "$given_ceiling" || exit 1
      sizing_say
      sized_units "''${given[0]}" "''${given[1]}" "''${given[2]}"
    '';
  };

  # `.#sized-build`: `nix build`, sized. What it is given is evaluated once
  # (`nix path-info --derivation`); of the units of `plan` (`unitPlan`), only
  # what Nix's own plan for that build lists (a dry run, `sizing_will_build`)
  # is built, each unit by `sized_units` — the test runs, the plan's lines
  # from `lateFrom` on, only once every compilation has ended, as in the
  # batteries —; then what it was given, sized as what is no unit — less,
  # after a unit failed under `--keep-going`, what needs a unit that was
  # not built, which is said and never built. Never the `.drv` closure: it
  # names every unit below a program the store already holds, and building
  # those would compile what the build itself never needs. The evaluation
  # and the dry run run in the background, waited for: a signal stops them
  # at once, where in the foreground the trap waits for them to end.
  sizedBuild =
    plan:
    writeShellApplication {
      name = "ronoctua-sized-build";
      # `sizing_measure` sets the numbers, where shellcheck cannot follow.
      excludeShellChecks = [ "SC2154" ];
      runtimeInputs = [
        coreutils
        gawk
        gnugrep
        gnused
      ];
      text = ''
        # shellcheck source=/dev/null
        . ${functions}
        sizing_stop_on_signals
        usage() {
          echo "usage: nix run .#sized-build -- [-L] [--keep-going] [--option NAME VALUE] [--no-link | --out-link PATH] INSTALLABLE…"
          echo "builds the installables sized to this machine: every unit they reach that Nix would build"
          echo "its own build, as many at once as their classes' slots fit, the heavy ones alone; then the rest"
        }
        # The options it knows: the out-link's, which only the last build
        # takes, and Nix's settings and logging, which every call takes. Any
        # other is refused, never guessed at (it might take a value).
        sized_flags=() link=() wanted=()
        while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
          case "$1" in
            -L | --print-build-logs | --fallback | --show-trace | --impure | \
              --offline | --refresh | -v | --verbose) sized_flags+=("$1") ;;
            --keep-going) sized_flags+=("$1"); sizing_keep_going=1 ;;
            --option)
              [ $# -ge 3 ] || { echo "ronoctua-sized-build: --option needs a name and a value" >&2; exit 2; }
              sized_flags+=("$1" "$2" "$3")
              shift 2
              ;;
            --no-link) link=(--no-link) ;;
            --out-link)
              [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "ronoctua-sized-build: --out-link needs a path" >&2; exit 2; }
              link=(--out-link "$2")
              shift
              ;;
            -h | --help) usage; exit 0 ;;
            --) shift; wanted+=("$@"); break ;;
            -*) echo "ronoctua-sized-build: $1 is not an option this takes (see --help)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
            *) wanted+=("$1") ;;
          esac
          shift
        done
        [ "''${#wanted[@]}" -gt 0 ] || { usage >&2; exit 2; }

        sizing_measure || exit 1
        sizing_say
        echo ""
        # Not `held`: `sized_units` has a local of that name, and a signal
        # inside it would have the `EXIT` trap read that one instead.
        sized_dir=$(mktemp -d)
        trap 'rm -rf "$sized_dir"' EXIT

        # WHAT IT IS GIVEN, evaluated once: every later build names `.drv`s,
        # which needs no evaluator.
        nix path-info --derivation "''${sized_flags[@]}" "''${wanted[@]}" > "$sized_dir/evaluated" &
        wait "$!" || exit 1
        mapfile -t drvs < "$sized_dir/evaluated"
        [ "''${#drvs[@]}" -gt 0 ] || { echo "ronoctua-sized-build: nothing to build" >&2; exit 1; }
        # WHAT THEIR BUILD WILL BUILD, in Nix's own plan — asked, and never
        # assumed: an answer lost or unread here would build the heavy units
        # beside everything else.
        nix build --dry-run "''${sized_flags[@]}" "''${drvs[@]/%/^*}" > /dev/null 2> "$sized_dir/dry" &
        if ! wait "$!"; then
          cat "$sized_dir/dry" >&2
          echo "ronoctua-sized-build: the dry run did not say what the build builds" >&2
          exit 1
        fi
        if ! sizing_will_build < "$sized_dir/dry" > "$sized_dir/will"; then
          cat "$sized_dir/dry" >&2
          echo "ronoctua-sized-build: the dry run said what it builds in words this does not read" >&2
          exit 1
        fi

        # A FAILED UNIT stops it, as a failed `nix build` stops; with
        # `--keep-going` the rest is still built — never what needs a unit
        # that was not built —, and the answer is still no. A plan or a dry
        # run it does not read builds nothing at all.
        failed=0
        echo "== the units it reaches =="
        # The test runs, the plan's last lines, after every compilation: a
        # test that measures time does not measure a compiler beside it.
        # shellcheck disable=SC2034
        sizing_late_from=${toString plan.lateFrom}
        if sized_units ${plan.text} "$sized_dir/will" "$sized_dir/units"; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi
        if [ "$rc" -eq 2 ] || { [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] && [ "''${sizing_keep_going:-0}" != 1 ]; }; then
          exit 1
        fi
        targets=("''${drvs[@]}")
        if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
          failed=1
          printf '%s\n' "''${drvs[@]}" > "$sized_dir/targets"
          if ! sizing_needing "$sized_dir/units/not-built" "$sized_dir/targets" > "$sized_dir/blocked"; then
            echo "ronoctua-sized-build: the store did not say what needs the units that were not built: nothing more is built" >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          targets=()
          for d in "''${drvs[@]}"; do
            if grep -qxF -- "$d" "$sized_dir/blocked"; then
              echo "not built: $d needs a unit that was not built above"
            else
              targets+=("$d")
            fi
          done
        fi

        echo "== what it was given =="
        if [ "''${#targets[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
          nix build "''${link[@]}" "''${sized_flags[@]}" \
            --max-jobs "$jobs" --cores "$cores" "''${targets[@]/%/^*}" &
          wait "$!" || exit 1
        fi
        exit "$failed"
      '';
    };
in
{
  inherit
    numbers
    classData
    classesFile
    classesOf
    plannedKinds
    unitPlan
    functions
    program
    unitsProgram
    sizedBuild
    ;
}
